media: mediatek: add the MT6897 video decoder

A V4L2 stateful decoder on the stock video firmware of the MT6897. The
firmware runs on the video coprocessor (mt6897-vcp) and drives the
decoder hardware itself. The kernel opens a session with it, hands it
bitstream and frame buffers, gives it the working memory it asks for and
keeps power and clocks of the hardware on while a session is open.

Messages and structures follow the vendor kernel for this SoC
(vdec_ipi_msg.h, vcodec_ipi_msg.h), which differ from those of the
MT6895. What was learned on a Lenovo TB375FC:

- The bitstream and the working memory the firmware reads itself come
  from the coprocessor's side of the IOMMU; frame buffers and the
  hardware's working memory from the decoder device.
- The firmware raises the decoder clock for every frame by switching the
  mux itself. It has to be told that the kernel uses the mux (the clock
  scaling message the vendor clock driver sends on prepare), the gate of
  the mux is opened through the hardware voter, and the PLL of the fast
  source is kept running. Which of the three it cannot do without was
  not separated. All three are done here until the clock driver models
  this mux as the vendor does.
- The firmware asks the voltage request block (DVFSRC) for a voltage
  level before a frame and waits without end if that block is off, so
  the driver does not probe before it.
- By default the firmware writes compressed frames (HyFBC). With two
  entries in its property table it writes uncompressed 16x32 blocks
  (MM21). They are copied to the application as NV12.
- A session that still holds frame buffers cannot be closed: a frame
  buffer message without a buffer empties the firmware's queue first.

One session at a time, H.264 only, and a change of the picture size in
the middle of a stream is not handled yet. Power and clocks stay on for
the whole session.

Tested on a TB375FC: a 640x360 H.264 file of eight frames decodes to the
same bytes as with FFmpeg.
